it was a story straight out of a spy  novel a mysterious weapon used by  hostile agents to target u.s diplomats  around the world a previously unknown  technology in the hands of the enemies  of the free world and it even has a name  straight from the thriller section  the havana syndrome  back in 2016 u.s staff of the american  embassy in cuba started complaining of  severe headaches nausea and other  symptoms after hearing high pitched  sounds sound an official report backed  up reports of brain damage possibly  caused by a sonic weapon the syndrome  has been reported from various locations  across the globe since then  this has become a political issue as  well politicians have pointed their  finger at russia as possibly being  behind the attacks christopher miller  the former acting defense secretary  under donald trump called the suspected  directed energy attacks and quote act of  war but now in the latest twist in this  saga a large-scale cia report into the  havana syndrome reportedly says that  those suffering from the condition were  not targeted in a global campaign by a  foreign power in fact it concludes that  the vast majority of cases were the  result of stress or other conventional  medical explanations  when this story first broke many  scientists warned that the most likely  explanation was mass psychogenic illness  or what is commonly known as mass  hysteria  two of them wrote this book together  havana syndrome mass psychogenic illness  and the real story behind the embassy  mystery and hysteria  and tonight i've got both of those  authors with me i'd like to welcome  robert w balo he's emeritus professor of  neurology at the ronald reagan ucla  medical center he joins me tonight from  los angeles and robert bartholomew he's  honorary senior lecturer in the  department of psychological medicine at  the university of auckland in new  zealand to both of you gentlemen it's  good to have you with us dr balor let me  start with you it seems the cia now  agrees that there's less to this story  than some  believe what's your take  well thank you for having me brent um  well my take is this is what we've been  saying all along uh  certainly  we expected that  this would be the final conclusion if it  is a final conclusion obviously this  report is  is  considered preliminary there's a lot of  backlash there's a lot of political  implications  but i certainly agree with what they  concluded i mean we right from the  beginning uh felt that we have to be  very  cautious and and  consider all possibilities but now we're  five years into this  and there's absolutely no evidence of  any kind of secret weapon  uh or that any foreign agent is  following our  embassy employees all over around the  world in fact every every continent now  has  has had uh some reports of of the  so-called havana syndrome  so uh  i believe do you feel vindicated dr  ballard do you and dr bartholomew do you  feel vindicated here  i think to some degree because uh we  took uh you know a lot of criticism i  mean i think one of the key things and i  and i them just emphasize this right  from the beginning  is that you know these people have real  symptoms and and you know i i have  compassion for them is i'm i'm  definitely not saying  that  that they're imagining their symptoms  that are this is not real the symptoms  are real but i think telling them they  had brain damage  is by no means uh you know  in their favor and indeed if you wanted  to pick something that would make it  unlikely that they're going to get  better it would be to  tell them that they have brain damage  dr bartholomew how does this actually  work i mean what are the dynamics behind  mass psychogenic illness or mass  hysteria  well think of mass psychogenic illness  as the placebo effect in reverse in  psychology it's known as the nocebo  effect based on negative expectations  this entire case  can be  summarized in one sentence  when you hear the sound of hoof beats in  the night  first think horses  not zebras the doctors at the state  department went for the most exotic  hypothesis early on  they went on a search for unicorns when  they should have stuck to mundane  explanations and people say things like  well the us government's been  experimenting with microwaves for years  that may be true but the us government  also experimented for 20 years trying to  develop psychic soldiers doing remote  viewing and how did that work out it  didn't work out very well just because  they're experimenting with it doesn't  mean it's true because there's something  called the laws of physics and the other  thing i'd like to point out here is that  this shifted from cuba  to other parts of the world later on  because the state department and the  department of defense issued alerts  around the world to be on the lookout  for anomalous health incidents and then  people all over the world started  reporting anomalous health incidents  because people have unusual health  incidents all the time and just because  they have a health incident doesn't mean  it's mass psychogenic illness a number  of these cases are people simply waking  up in the morning having a headache or  feeling unusual and attributing it to  this new condition havana syndrome what  dr bartholomew let me just ask you then  what what do you think prompted these  physicians when these cases were first  reported what made these physicians  think of the zebra instead of the horse  uh incompetence really and look the you  have the national academy of sciences uh  panel no one invited us to  present on that panel  you had a panel that was essentially  loaded with believers and not skeptics  this is a clear case  of politics being mixed with science and  whenever that happens the outcome is not  good i'm sure professor balo and i would  love to speak before the senate foreign  relations committee and explain more as  to what's been going on here and the  other issue here is the symptoms of  havana syndrome are so vague as to be  experienced by  any human being who's ever lived in any  week of their life headache nausea  dizziness  difficulty concentrating forgetfulness  confusion tinnitus insomnia and so when  you issue an alert around the world for  symptoms of havana syndrome and they're  so vague you are going to get uh a  deluge of people reporting similar  symptoms well let me ask dr bella about  this i mean we did see studies backing  up claims of directed energy  injuries  what do you make of them  well  they were false uh simply it was leaks  to the press initially that there were  mri abnormalities that that would be a  really  important and significant finding but  when the actual data was presented  it turns out that the mri scans of the  brain were normal there were a few  non-specific changes that we would  expect to see in any group of people of  that size and so the whole idea of mri  abnormalities was was  just bogus and then the the testing that  they reported that suggests there was  brain injury or  brain impairment where these were  non-specific tests tests for example eye  tracking and reaction time things  that  anybody with anxiety would show the same  abnormalities and they really didn't  have an actual adequate control group so  the data was flawed right from the  beginning i've got about a minute left  dr  i'll let you have this last question i  mean  how often does this phenomenon occur i  mean is this rare or is this something  that keeps popping up all the time  look there was a long history of mass  psychogenic illness and it's very much  misunderstood you know there's a problem  when pamela spratlin the former head of  the bible panel looking into this  was interviewing havana syndrome victims  and said you know i am considering the  possibility of mass psychogenic illness  and she was forced to resign because of  the public outcry and i'll leave you  with this yes a line from shakespeare a  midsummer night's dream or in the night  imagining some fear how easy is a bush  supposed a bear and in this case we're  looking at the russian bear it's all  psychology  yeah  and it's fascinating too and we are  grateful that both of you gentlemen took  the time out especially on this friday  dr bartholomew i think for you at  saturday morning to talk with us um this  is enlightening and this is an important  discussion appreciate both of you  talking with us tonight thank you  thank you
 

